is flagged on unchanged data by the tests alone. \code{drftcal()} measures
this for the table at hand: it splits \code{data} at random into two halves
\code{n} times, runs \code{drfttab()} on each pair, and reports the share of
splits on which each column, and any column, was flagged. Each half has
half the rows, so the rates describe a comparison at that size.

